WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.286 --> 00:00:05.125
Although Marius was an attorney, he was not interested in practicing law.

2
00:00:05.126 --> 00:00:09.372
It was tiring to think of dealing with lawyers and attending court.

3
00:00:09.373 --> 00:00:14.213
Of course, he could probably make more money as a lawyer than as a translator.

4
00:00:14.214 --> 00:00:20.880
But the publishing house provided him with steady work that left him with enough free time to do as he pleased.

5
00:00:20.881 --> 00:00:30.023
Marius delighted in taking long walks along the outer boulevards of Paris or on the quiet paths of the Luxembourg Gardens, a large park.

6
00:00:30.024 --> 00:00:34.682
He sometimes spent half a day doing nothing but admiring the gardens.

7
00:00:34.683 --> 00:00:39.428
It was during one of these walks that he discovered the old Gorbeau house.

8
00:00:39.429 --> 00:00:44.261
Its isolation and cheapness attracted him, so he rented a room there.

9
00:00:44.262 --> 00:00:51.086
Marius spent so much time outdoors that he was barely aware that he had neighbors.

10
00:00:51.087 --> 00:01:02.332
One day in the summer of 1831, the housekeeper mentioned that Marius’neighbors, the wretched Jondrette family, were about to be thrown out into the street.

11
00:01:02.333 --> 00:01:05.237
"Why is that?" he asked the woman.

12
00:01:05.238 --> 00:01:10.904
"Because they don’t pay their rent. They’re behind by at least several months."

13
00:01:10.905 --> 00:01:12.889
"How much does that amount to?"

14
00:01:12.890 --> 00:01:16.078
"Twenty francs," she replied.

15
00:01:16.079 --> 00:01:19.321
Marius had thirty francs in reserve in a drawer.

16
00:01:19.322 --> 00:01:25.150
He didn’t know these people at all, but at this point even he had more money than they did.

17
00:01:25.151 --> 00:01:30.784
Marius was quite a kind person, so he said, "I’ll give you 25 francs.

18
00:01:30.785 --> 00:01:34.976
Take out twenty for the rent and give the extra five to the Jondrettes.

19
00:01:34.977 --> 00:01:37.818
But don’t tell them that the money came from me."

20
00:01:37.819 --> 00:01:43.302
Marius was now a fine-looking young man of medium height with jet-black hair.

21
00:01:43.303 --> 00:01:49.468
At the time of his worst poverty, he noticed girls looking at him when he passed.

22
00:01:49.469 --> 00:01:54.793
He could feel them staring and he guessed that they were laughing at his old clothes.

23
00:01:54.794 --> 00:01:59.619
But they were really staring after him because he was so handsome.

24
00:01:59.620 --> 00:02:06.198
As Marius walked through the Luxembourg Gardens, he always saw the same couple sitting on the same bench:

25
00:02:06.199 --> 00:02:12.436
an older man with white hair and a girl whom Marius assumed was the man’s daughter.

26
00:02:12.437 --> 00:02:20.507
Marius began thinking of the man as Monsieur Leblanc because he had white hair, and blanc means "white" in French.

27
00:02:20.508 --> 00:02:29.269
The girl was not very pretty. Thin and awkward, almost ugly, she wore an ill-fitting dress of black wool and a velvet hat.

28
00:02:29.270 --> 00:02:34.015
Then Marius did not walk in the park for about six months.

29
00:02:34.016 --> 00:02:42.682
When he returned, Monsieur Leblanc was still sitting on the same bench, but now the girl next to him was truly beautiful.

30
00:02:42.683 --> 00:02:47.253
At first Marius thought she must be the original girl’s sister.

31
00:02:47.254 --> 00:02:53.666
But he soon realized that this lovely creature with the brown hair and blue eyes was the same girl.

32
00:02:53.667 --> 00:03:02.983
One day Marius walked past her and their eyes met. The next morning he put on his new coat, trousers, hat, and boots.

33
00:03:02.984 --> 00:03:11.809
In the park he instantly spotted the girl and her father. Marius did not dare to look at her as he passed, but he blushed deep red.

34
00:03:11.810 --> 00:03:17.801
The young man had fallen desperately in love, but he didn’t want to be too obvious.

35
00:03:17.802 --> 00:03:23.055
He didn’t parade in front of her anymore because he didn’t want to attract her father’s attention.

36
00:03:23.056 --> 00:03:28.888
Instead he went to the park and sat on a bench within sight of his new love.

37
00:03:28.889 --> 00:03:32.834
Meanwhile the girl had also noticed Marius.

38
00:03:32.835 --> 00:03:38.960
She turned on her bench, so that she could listen to her father and look at Marius at the same time.

39
00:03:38.961 --> 00:03:45.206
Sometimes her father got up and walked with the girl to another bench. Marius followed them.

40
00:03:45.207 --> 00:03:49.690
Sometimes the father began to come without the daughter.

41
00:03:49.691 --> 00:03:53.190
On those days Marius left the park early.

42
00:03:53.191 --> 00:03:59.004
One night Marius found a handkerchief on the bench where the father and daughter had just been sitting.

43
00:03:59.004 --> 00:04:02.698
It was marked with the initials U.F.

44
00:04:02.699 --> 00:04:08.341
"Her name must be Ursula," he said to himself. "What a sweet name!"

45
00:04:08.342 --> 00:04:16.095
At first it was enough for Marius to believe that he knew the girl’s name. He could live on that fact alone for weeks.

46
00:04:16.096 --> 00:04:21.912
But after a month, he wanted to know more about her. He wanted to know where she lived.

47
00:04:21.913 --> 00:04:26.912
He had already made one mistake by following the girl when she moved to another bench.

48
00:04:26.913 --> 00:04:31.866
Then he did not stay in the park when Monsieur Leblanc came without the girl.

49
00:04:31.867 --> 00:04:36.898
Now he made a third error: He followed Ursula home.

50
00:04:36.899 --> 00:04:44.239
She lived in the Rue de l’Ouest, in the least frequented part of the street, in a house of modest appearance.

51
00:04:44.240 --> 00:04:49.231
But this piece of knowledge only increased Marius’need for more information.

52
00:04:49.232 --> 00:04:57.231
One night after he had followed Ursula and Monsieur Leblanc home, Marius entered the building and talked to the porter.

53
00:04:57.232 --> 00:05:04.144
"Is that gentleman who just came in the one who lives on the second floor?" asked Marius, fishing for information.

54
00:05:04.145 --> 00:05:07.075
"No, he lives on the fourth floor."

55
00:05:07.076 --> 00:05:13.707
Another fact for Marius. His success made him bolder, and he dared to ask another question.

56
00:05:13.708 --> 00:05:17.287
"In the apartment with the windows facing the street?"

57
00:05:17.288 --> 00:05:21.100
"All the apartments face the street," replied the porter.

58
00:05:21.102 --> 00:05:24.541
"And what is this gentleman’s occupation?"

59
00:05:24.542 --> 00:05:27.882
"He lives on the income from his investments, monsieur.

60
00:05:27.883 --> 00:05:33.945
He’s a very kind man, who does a great deal of good among the poor even though he’s not rich."

61
00:05:33.946 --> 00:05:36.399
"What’s his name?" asked Marius.

62
00:05:36.400 --> 00:05:39.866
The porter studied Marius for a moment.

63
00:05:39.867 --> 00:05:42.517
"Are you a detective?"

64
00:05:42.518 --> 00:05:48.509
"Oh! No." Marius was too flustered to say anything more and left the building.

65
00:05:48.510 --> 00:05:53.509
But he was still in high spirits because he was making progress.

66
00:05:53.510 --> 00:05:59.322
The next day Monsieur Leblanc and his daughter made only a short visit to the Luxembourg Gardens.

67
00:05:59.324 --> 00:06:02.755
Marius followed them back to the Rue de l’Ouest.

68
00:06:02.756 --> 00:06:07.490
Upon reaching the door, Monsieur Leblanc let his daughter enter ahead of him.

69
00:06:07.491 --> 00:06:12.819
Then he turned around and looked steadily at Marius.

70
00:06:12.820 --> 00:06:22.502
Marius hurried away. To his dismay, they did not come to the park at all the next day. Marius waited in vain all afternoon.

71
00:06:22.503 --> 00:06:28.398
At nightfall he went to the Rue de l’Ouest and saw a light in the fourth-floor windows.

72
00:06:28.399 --> 00:06:35.978
He walked beneath these windows until the light was put out. He did not mind that he had missed dinner.

73
00:06:35.979 --> 00:06:38.732
Marius passed a week in this manner.

74
00:06:38.733 --> 00:06:46.978
Monsieur Leblanc and his daughter did not reappear in the park, but Marius did not dare to watch their building during the day.

75
00:06:46.979 --> 00:06:53.549
Sometimes at night he saw shadows moving against the blinds, and his heart beat quickly.

76
00:06:53.550 --> 00:06:57.534
On the eighth day there was no light in the windows.

77
00:06:57.535 --> 00:07:01.041
"Perhaps they’ve gone out," Marius said to himself.

78
00:07:01.042 --> 00:07:08.041
He waited until one o’clock in the morning, but no one entered the building and no light appeared at the windows.

79
00:07:08.042 --> 00:07:13.184
There was no light again the next night, so Marius knocked on the front door.

80
00:07:13.185 --> 00:07:17.938
"Has the gentleman on the fourth floor gone on vacation?" he asked the porter.

81
00:07:17.939 --> 00:07:21.103
"No, he moved out yesterday."

82
00:07:21.104 --> 00:07:27.023
Marius tottered, but managed to say weakly, "Where does he live now?"

83
00:07:27.024 --> 00:07:33.532
"I have no idea," said the porter, shutting the door. "He left no forwarding address."

